  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-MYS126</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Labour dimensions of social  security strengthened</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">From March 2024, the UNDP and the ILO through the Malaysia-UN SDG Trust Fund are pursing the joint initative to improve People with Disabilities&apos; access to open labour market with enhanced social protection.

Expected results: In respect of social protection coverage, the project will deliver recommnedationsrecommendations, practical next steps, facilitate dialogue, provide relevant international examples to ensure that coverage of PWDs can and will increase over the short and medium term. 

The broad Outputs of the project include: Output 1: Fact finding &#x2013; collection of relevant data and information to analyse the current situation and to allow a gap analysis to be undertaken. Recommendations on improving data will also be made at this stage; Output 2: Consultation &#x2013; discussion and interaction with relevant stakeholders to ensure that recommendations are appropriate and there is an appreciation of the positive impacts of inclusion of PWDs in employment and social protection schemes; Output 3 &#x2013; Recommendations and next steps based on Outputs 1 and 2. This will take various forms including reports, policy briefs, workshops and training. 

Regarding the expansion of beneficiaries under the social protection schemes, through the work of the Regional Acturial Services in Bangkok supporting Malaysia since 2022, it has resulted from 1 July 2024 that foreign workers have been covered under the invalidity and survivors&#x2019; scheme managed by PERKESO. This very welcome development - supported by the ILO - means that these workers are now covered under two of the three social security schemes in Malaysia managed by PERKESO, with coverage under the Employment Injury scheme being extended to them in 2019.</narrative>
  </description>
